---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';

const REPO = 'https://github.com/nightswatchhq/nuthatch';
const DS_REPO = 'https://github.com/nightswatchhq/nuthatch-ds';
const HORIZON_NEST = 'https://github.com/nightswatchhq/horizon-nest';
const DS_CONTRACT = 'https://arbiscan.io/address/0x647D1Fd14AF2DE3947522B74F1de5B99d317c10A';

// The right-hand column: each card is one concrete, checkable touchpoint. Keep the copy to two or
// three sentences; the argument lives on the left.
const meets = [
  {
    k: '01 · Horizon data service',
    tag: 'live',
    h: 'Nuthatch is a Graph Horizon data service',
    p: 'A nest behind a paid, TAP-gated route on Arbitrum One. A provider advertises a nest identity, a query mode and an endpoint on chain, so a consumer buys a reproducible dataset rather than a server that says it does SQL. The first offering is an index of Horizon itself, and one bounded payment has settled.',
    links: [
      { href: '/blog/nuthatch-data-service-on-graph-horizon', text: 'the write-up' },
      { href: DS_REPO, text: 'nuthatch-ds', ext: true },
      { href: DS_CONTRACT, text: 'the contract', ext: true },
    ],
  },
  {
    k: '02 · Worked example',
    tag: 'shipped',
    h: 'A Graph subgraph, rebuilt as a nest',
    p: 'The example this site walks through is a real Graph Horizon subgraph reproduced as a nest and checked field by field against the gateway. A public dashboard serves two of its panels from that nest today, next to the panels the gateway still serves.',
    links: [
      { href: '/example', text: 'the example' },
      { href: '/stories', text: 'the story' },
      { href: HORIZON_NEST, text: 'horizon-nest', ext: true },
    ],
  },
  {
    k: '03 · Subgraph fallback',
    tag: 'docs',
    h: 'A nest keeps a stuck subgraph’s data served',
    p: 'When a subgraph has no indexer, falls behind, or goes unreachable, the event-derived part of its data can keep being served from a nest until it recovers. The docs are about drawing that boundary before the outage rather than during it.',
    links: [
      { href: '/docs/build/subgraph-fallback', text: 'how to build one' },
      { href: '/blog/no-subgraph-no-problem', text: 'the first case' },
    ],
  },
  {
    k: '04 · init --from-subgraph',
    tag: 'shipped',
    h: 'A subgraph manifest scaffolds a nest',
    p: 'Point Nuthatch at a manifest CID and it maps each data source to a contract, vendors the ABIs the manifest pins, and carries the start blocks and event handlers across. It was the project’s first external contribution, from inside the Graph community.',
    links: [
      { href: REPO + '/issues/241', text: 'the contribution', ext: true },
      { href: '/roadmap', text: 'on the roadmap' },
    ],
  },
  {
    k: '05 · Hackathon bounty',
    tag: 'july 2026',
    h: 'The Graph named Nuthatch in a bounty',
    p: 'ETHGlobal Lisbon’s Best AI Tooling track listed a self-hosted indexer such as Nuthatch as an accepted data backend, alongside Subgraph Studio, in the qualification rules rather than the examples. Somebody wrote that sentence on purpose.',
    links: [
      { href: '/blog/the-graph-put-nuthatch-in-their-hackathon-bounty', text: 'the post' },
    ],
  },
];
---
<Layout
  path="/the-graph"
  title="Nuthatch & The Graph - Nuthatch"
  description="The Graph is a network and a market for indexed data. Nuthatch is a binary that produces it. Why the two complement rather than compete: the Horizon data service, the subgraph fallback, init --from-subgraph, and the hackathon bounty."
>
  <section class="page">
    <div class="wrap">
      <header class="head narrow">
        <p class="section-label">Nuthatch &amp; The Graph</p>
        <h1>Different jobs, same data</h1>
        <p class="lede">
          Nuthatch gets read, now and then, as a competitor to The Graph. It is not one, and the
          reason is structural rather than diplomatic. The Graph is a network: a market in which
          indexers serve subgraphs, consumers pay query fees, and the protocol arbitrates who did
          the work honestly. Nuthatch is a binary. It turns a contract address into an indexed API
          on whichever machine you happen to be sitting at. One is a place to buy indexed data. The
          other is a tool for producing it. Markets do not compete with tools; they run on them.
        </p>
      </header>

      <div class="split">
        <article class="argument">
          <h2>The self-hosting argument</h2>
          <p>
            Saying that Nuthatch competes with The Graph is a little like saying that graph-node
            does. Anyone has always been free to run their own graph-node, Postgres and IPFS and
            index their own subgraph on their own hardware, and nobody has ever called that a rival
            protocol. It is the protocol's own software, self-hosted. Nuthatch is the same shape
            with the operational weight taken out: one static binary, an embedded store, no Docker,
            nothing to rent. A team that runs a nest for the contracts it wrote is not a lost
            customer of the network, any more than a team running its own graph-node is. Most of
            them were never going to buy that index from anyone.
          </p>
          <p>
            The two projects also agree about the thing that matters. The Graph's answer to a
            centralised data provider is to decentralise who serves you. Nuthatch's answer is that,
            for the contracts you own, you need not be served at all. Both are ways of not trusting
            one company's endpoint. Neither is wrong, and they are not exclusive: run your own nest
            for the protocol you build, and query the network for the fifteen thousand contracts
            you do not.
          </p>
          <p>
            And the people. Nuthatch was written by people who spent years inside The Graph's
            ecosystem and still work there. The front page carries a comparison table with The
            Graph in it, and it states facts rather than a verdict, because the authors know rather
            precisely what the network does well.
          </p>

          <h2>Where they part ways</h2>
          <p>
            Honesty about the seam is the point of this page, so here is what Nuthatch is not. It
            is not a network. There is no market, no curation, no query fees in the binary, and no
            dispute process; verification means re-executing deterministic code and getting the
            same bytes, which is a strong property for one operator and a different property from
            a decentralised one. It does not speak GraphQL yet; a compatibility layer for
            subgraph-shaped clients is <a href="/roadmap">on the roadmap</a> and not in the
            release. Contract reads and IPFS documents are pinned and verified rather than fetched
            freely, so a subgraph that leans on either needs its query surface reviewed rather than
            assumed. And it does not pretend to serve a hundred thousand subgraphs to strangers
            from one box. If your application reads from protocols you do not own, across chains
            you do not run nodes for, the network is the right tool, and Nuthatch will not argue.
          </p>

          <h2>What comes next</h2>
          <p>
            Two pieces of work point the same way. A hosted nests data service, some of it
            community-driven, so that a team which does not want to operate a box can still buy a
            nest through the protocol rather than from a private vendor. And the GraphQL
            compatibility layer, so a subgraph-shaped client can point at a nest without being
            rewritten. Neither has a date, because the roadmap promises order rather than dates.
            Both make a nest a better citizen of the network, not a way around it.
          </p>

          <p class="signoff">
            Run your own nest for the contracts you own. Query the network for everything else.
            <a href="/install">Install it</a>, or
            <a href={DS_REPO} rel="noopener">read the data service</a>.
          </p>
        </article>

        <aside class="meets" aria-labelledby="meets-h">
          <p class="section-label" id="meets-h">Where they already meet</p>
          {meets.map((m) => (
            <div class="meet">
              <div class="meet-top">
                <span class="meet-k">{m.k}</span>
                <span class="tag meet-tag">{m.tag}</span>
              </div>
              <h3>{m.h}</h3>
              <p>{m.p}</p>
              <p class="meet-links">
                {m.links.map((l) => (
                  <a href={l.href} rel={l.ext ? 'noopener' : undefined}>{l.text} →</a>
                ))}
              </p>
            </div>
          ))}
        </aside>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>

<style>
  .page { padding-block: clamp(3rem, 6vw, 4.5rem) clamp(4rem, 9vw, 6.5rem); }
  .head h1 { margin-bottom: 1.6rem; }
  .lede { color: var(--text); font-size: 1.12rem; line-height: 1.7; }

  .split {
    display: grid; grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1.1fr) minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: clamp(2.5rem, 5vw, 4.5rem);
    margin-top: clamp(3rem, 6vw, 4.5rem);
    align-items: start;
  }

  .argument h2 { font-size: 1.35rem; margin: 0 0 0.9rem; }
  .argument h2 + p { margin-top: 0; }
  .argument p { color: var(--text-muted); font-size: 1.02rem; line-height: 1.72; margin-bottom: 1.2rem; }
  .argument p:last-of-type { margin-bottom: 0; }
  .argument h2:not(:first-child) { margin-top: 2.4rem; }
  .signoff { color: var(--text) !important; font-weight: 500; margin-top: 2rem !important; }

  .meets { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 0.9rem; }
  .meets .section-label { margin-bottom: 0.2rem; }
  .meet {
    background: var(--bg-elev); border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: var(--radius);
    padding: 1.15rem 1.25rem 1.05rem;
    transition: border-color 0.18s var(--ease);
  }
  .meet:hover { border-color: var(--border-hi); }
  .meet-top { display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: space-between; gap: 0.6rem; margin-bottom: 0.55rem; }
  .meet-k {
    font-family: 'JetBrains Mono', monospace; font-size: 0.7rem; letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--text-faint);
  }
  .meet-tag { background: var(--pale-rust-bg); color: var(--rust); }
  .meet h3 { font-size: 1.05rem; margin-bottom: 0.45rem; }
  .meet p { color: var(--text-muted); font-size: 0.92rem; line-height: 1.6; }
  .meet-links { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 0.3rem 1.1rem; margin-top: 0.7rem; }
  .meet-links a { font-family: 'JetBrains Mono', monospace; font-size: 0.8rem; }

  @media (max-width: 56rem) {
    .split { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
  }
</style>
